#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161968 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161760 :question/id #uuid "f9912357-1d1d-5413-917e-257dc5dfc0fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-8051" :question/problem "\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} \\\\ 8 & 3 & {\\color{blue}-4} \\\\ 9 & 7 & {\\color{blue}-2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} \\\\ -16 & -17 & {\\color{blue}-4} \\\\ -3 & -3 & {\\color{blue}-2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_3,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of $a_{13}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}: &={C}_{1}+6{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}: &={C}_{2}+5{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -16 & -17 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $C_1:=C_1+6C_3\\quad$ and $\\quad C_2:=C_2+5C_3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85e1fe3f-e85e-5ab4-a594-c55b84bb789a" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674183 :question-generator/id #uuid "d59ba18e-f6c4-5c63-bf62-5221949ce48f" :question-generator/script "generators/ec/ec9cbe7a88642cc6fa459989c70caf5be34e2f48693fa631c023e168121c8066/17592186161968.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a941c4e1-5e36-53ce-bc6